{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>After I find favor in his eyes.</b> Some say that the vav in <b>in Boaz</b> refers back to Boaz previously mentioned, and the meaning is perhaps 'and he will be.' In my opinion, the vav in <b>in</b> [refers to] the owner of the field, and if he was not mentioned, it is on account of 'and chance happened' (ויקר מקרה), and its meaning is thus: he was providentially encountered." "<b>YHWH be with you.</b> To help you, because they are laboring." "<b>May YHWH bless you.</b> That He give blessing in the harvest."], :book :ruth, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 2, :notes {0 "The phrase ויקר מקרה quotes Ruth 2:3; Ibn Ezra argues the suffix in 'in his eyes' is an indefinite/pronominal reference to whoever's field she happens to be in, resolved by providence."}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Ruth -- Daat" "http://www.daat.ac.il/he-il/tanach/parshanut_hamikra/full-text/eben-ezra/ruth"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T19:43:25.208252489Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}